WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.420
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:05.414 --> 00:00:08.220
5: How a Dharma Gets Under Your Skin.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.720
Why does a dharma feel, from the inside,

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.840
not like a belief you hold but like
reality itself?

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.980
Because it was never installed as a
belief.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.620
It was grooved into your gut, your habits,
and the wiring of your brain,

7
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.220
below the reach of the part of you that
could doubt it.

8
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.140
A man who has not believed in God for
thirty years stands at his mother's funeral,

9
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.120
and as the old words begin,

10
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.820
the ones he was raised on and long ago
decided were false, he finds,

11
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.380
to his irritation and surprise, that his
throat closes and his eyes sting.

12
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.280
Or: a woman who can give a crisp, confident
argument for why no food is unclean,

13
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.760
why it is all just protein and habit and
ancient plumbing rules,

14
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.580
cannot, when it comes to it, bring the
forbidden thing to her lips.

15
00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:58.740
Her hand will not do it.

16
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.760
Or, simpler still: you feel the back of
your neck prickle

17
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.520
when a flag goes up and a crowd goes
quiet,

18
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.780
and some part of you stands a little
straighter,

19
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.440
whatever your politics, whatever you think
you think.

20
00:01:12.254 --> 00:01:14.300
In each case something is running a
program

21
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.620
the conscious mind has formally cancelled.

22
00:01:17.169 --> 00:01:20.220
The argument has been won, on paper, by
the sceptic.

23
00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.260
And the body was not listening.

24
00:01:22.814 --> 00:01:25.500
There is a self underneath the self that
was speaking,

25
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.680
with its own convictions, and it does not
answer to reason.

26
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.200
That underneath-self is where a dharma
actually lives.

27
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.300
In the first four parts we have looked at
dharmas from the outside,

28
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.740
as made things, plural things, things with
histories and functions.

29
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.240
But Part 4 ended on a puzzle.

30
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.680
From the inside, a dharma never feels
made, or chosen, or cultural.

31
00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:55.120
It feels like simply the truth, as plain
and unarguable as the floor being solid.

32
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.500
The question is how that happens:

33
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.480
how a thing that human beings built gets

34
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.980
so deep inside other human beings that it
stops feeling built at all.

35
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.340
To answer, we have to stop asking what a
dharma teaches

36
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.640
and start asking what it does to you --

37
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.840
down through three layers: the gut, the
body, and the brain itself.

38
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.180
The elephant and the rider

39
00:02:20.556 --> 00:02:24.780
There is a flattering story we tell about
ourselves, and it is mostly wrong.

40
00:02:25.097 --> 00:02:28.560
The story is that we are reasoning
creatures who examine the evidence,

41
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.720
weigh the arguments, and arrive at our
moral convictions as conclusions.

42
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.400
On this picture, we believe cruelty is
wrong because we have thought it through.

43
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.420
The psychologist Jonathan Haidt has spent
a career showing

44
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.200
that this is almost backwards,

45
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.640
and he has a useful image for the truth.

46
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.300
The mind, he says, is like a small rider
on the back of a very large elephant.

47
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.940
The elephant is everything fast and
automatic in us,

48
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.560
our instincts and gut reactions; the rider
is conscious reasoning.

49
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.280
We imagine the rider is steering.

50
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.960
In fact, most of the time, the elephant
leans where it was always going to lean,

51
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.880
and the rider's real job is to come up
with a respectable reason after the fact.

52
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.120
In Haidt's blunter phrase,

53
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.120
moral judgement is "the emotional dog and
its rational tail."

54
00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:20.400
The feeling comes first, fast and certain,

55
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.600
and the justification trots along behind

56
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.060
to explain a verdict that was already
reached.

57
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.460
If that is right, then the interesting
question

58
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.900
about any dharma is what it does to the
elephant.

59
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.380
Haidt has a second idea that matters as
much.

60
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.380
The elephant, he proposes, comes equipped
with several moral senses,

61
00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.380
a small set of evolved "foundations" he
likes

62
00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.640
to call the taste buds of the moral mind.

63
00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.740
There is a taste bud for care, that flares
at cruelty and suffering.

64
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.960
One for fairness, that flares at cheating.

65
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.660
One for liberty, that flares at being
dominated.

66
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.180
And then a cluster he calls the binding
foundations: loyalty,

67
00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.720
that prizes the group and hates the
traitor; authority,

68
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.360
that respects rank and order; and
sanctity,

69
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.260
that recoils from the degraded and the
impure and guards the holy.

70
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.740
Working across cultures,

71
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:20.480
the anthropologist Richard Shweder found
the same spread under three broad headings:

72
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.000
an ethics of autonomy (the language of
harm, rights, and fairness to individuals),

73
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.000
an ethics of community (the language of
duty,

74
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.200
loyalty, hierarchy, and one's place in a
whole),

75
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.700
and an ethics of divinity (the language of
purity, sanctity, and the sacred order).

76
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.920
Most of the modern West speaks fluent
autonomy

77
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.720
and finds the other two faintly
embarrassing or sinister.

78
00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.060
Most human cultures, across most of
history,

79
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.440
have spoken all three as a matter of
course.

80
00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.700
When someone formed by an ethics of
divinity

81
00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.160
and someone formed by an ethics of
autonomy

82
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.600
argue about what may be done with a body,

83
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.100
or a flag, or a holy book,

84
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.780
they are not drawing different conclusions
from shared premises.

85
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.820
They are speaking different moral
languages,

86
00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.540
each fluent, each half-deaf to the other.

87
00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.360
Every human being has all of these, more
or less,

88
00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.980
but no two cultures cook with them the
same way.

89
00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.860
So a dharma is, in large part,

90
00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.940
a particular recipe written over those
shared taste buds:

91
00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.460
the first layer of how it gets inside you.

92
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.380
It cranks some up and mutes others.

93
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.700
One tradition builds its whole moral world
on sanctity and loyalty and authority,

94
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.460
so that purity and faithfulness

95
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.480
and proper order feel like the very
substance of goodness.

96
00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.120
Another builds almost everything on care
and fairness,

97
00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.320
so that those same concerns for purity

98
00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.300
and hierarchy come to feel faintly
disgusting.

99
00:05:48.145 --> 00:05:51.220
The people raised in those two dharmas
are, mostly,

100
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.160
tasting the same situation with
differently tuned tongues,

101
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.900
and then their riders are manufacturing
the arguments.

102
00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.500
This is why moral disagreement across
dharmas is so strangely hopeless,

103
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.680
so prone to each side concluding the other
is worse than wrong: depraved.

104
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.300
One cannot argue someone into a taste they
do not have.

105
00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.800
Though I should be straight about how firm
that map is.

106
00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.460
The taste-bud image is vivid and useful,

107
00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.960
but the specific list has never been
stable,

108
00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.940
and Haidt has always said so, asking openly
how many foundations there really are.

109
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.640
Since then the picture has grown shakier
rather than firmer.

110
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.540
When researchers test whether the six
behave like

111
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.540
six distinct things in how people actually
answer,

112
00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.780
the model does not fit; the versions that
do fit come out with a different set,

113
00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.640
and more often than not with seven.

114
00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.140
Sanctity is the weakest of the lot,

115
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.680
used so many different ways across studies

116
00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.820
that one systematic review concluded it is
not a single thing at all,

117
00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.160
but a bundle of themes held together only
by not being obvious harm.

118
00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:57.980
Haidt's own team has since revised the
theory and split fairness in two.

119
00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.940
There is also a rival account.

120
00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.620
Back in Part 1 we met Oliver Scott Curry's
argument

121
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.960
that morality is a set of solutions to the
recurring problems of cooperation.

122
00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.520
When Curry's team put the two frameworks
head to head,

123
00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.320
testing each against people's real moral
judgements,

124
00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.420
the cooperation model fitted and Haidt's
did not.

125
00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.580
I am not going to try to resolve that
fight,

126
00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.480
but I notice what the disagreement is
actually about:

127
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.840
the inventory, not the thing the inventory
is for.

128
00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.520
Both camps hold that there is a small set

129
00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.220
of moral concerns humans everywhere run
on,

130
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.200
that we do not learn them from scratch,

131
00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.720
and that different cultures crank them to
wildly different settings.

132
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.420
So take the taste buds as a working
vocabulary rather than a parts list.

133
00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.540
The sharpest name for this clash is the
philosopher Joshua Greene's.

134
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.260
There are, he says, two quite different
moral tragedies.

135
00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:59.880
The first is the old one a dharma exists
to solve: me against us,

136
00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.840
the selfish individual tempted to cheat
the group,

137
00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.520
which every working ethic labours to
overcome.

138
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.280
The second is the one that undoes us now,

139
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.720
and a dharma cannot solve it, because a
dharma is it.

140
00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.360
Greene calls it the tragedy of commonsense
morality:

141
00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.040
not me against us, but us against them,

142
00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.300
two groups each equipped with a moral
system that is internally coherent,

143
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:25.440
deeply felt, and flatly incompatible with
the other's.

144
00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.020
Each side experiences its own parochial
intuition as plain,

145
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.440
self-evident right, and the other's as
obvious wickedness.

146
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.660
In truth, to put it with some cruelty,

147
00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.940
what each is calling "right" is often just

148
00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:42.420
after-the-fact rationalisation slathered
over a self-serving,

149
00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:43.900
group-bound gut feeling.

150
00:08:44.999 --> 00:08:49.360
Two dharmas meeting at a border do not
feel to those involved like two recipes.

151
00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:53.740
They feel like good meeting evil, from
both sides at once.

152
00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.000
Whose gut?

153
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.440
Most people reading this, like me, have a
moral palate that runs heavily on care,

154
00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.700
fairness, and liberty, and rather lightly
on sanctity,

155
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.840
loyalty, and deference to authority.

156
00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.620
That particular setting feels, from the
inside,

157
00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.140
not like one recipe among many but like
the absence of recipe, like plain,

158
00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.660
neutral, grown-up human decency,

159
00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.980
the place one ends up on giving up
superstition

160
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.240
and tribe and simply thinking clearly.

161
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.840
That feeling is itself the thing to be
suspicious of.

162
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.000
Joseph Henrich and his fellow
anthropologists gave

163
00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:37.080
the relevant population a deliberately
rude acronym:

164
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:42.740
WEIRD, for Western, Educated,
Industrialised, Rich, and Democratic.

165
00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:44.240
These are the people on

166
00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.620
whom the science of human psychology was
overwhelmingly built,

167
00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:51.000
because they are the people who happened
to be standing near the universities.

168
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.920
And the discovery, made over years and
across domain after domain,

169
00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.940
is that WEIRD people are not a
representative sample of humanity at all.

170
00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:05.340
On how they treat strangers, on whether
they see the world in wholes or in parts,

171
00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:09.720
on whether they define themselves as
individuals or as threads in a family,

172
00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.040
even on which way they fall for certain
optical illusions,

173
00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.680
the modern Western mind turns out to be a
global outlier,

174
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.020
one of the least typical varieties of
human being on the planet.

175
00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.980
The morality that feels like the human
default is a local product,

176
00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.500
and a statistically peculiar one.

177
00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.520
The same parochialism hides inside even
our theories of moral growth.

178
00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:36.500
The most famous account of how children
develop morally,

179
00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:41.200
Lawrence Kohlberg's, arranges moral
reasoning into a ladder of stages,

180
00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.800
climbing from a childish "avoid
punishment,"

181
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.620
through "follow the rules of my group," up

182
00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.900
to a supposed summit of abstract universal
principle:

183
00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:54.640
the lone conscience that can defy an
unjust law in the name of pure justice.

184
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.200
It is an appealing picture,

185
00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.240
until one notices that the summit looks

186
00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.200
suspiciously like a Western philosophy
professor,

187
00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:03.040
and that the people

188
00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.500
who reliably score highest on it are the
educated Western individualists

189
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.560
who designed the test.

190
00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:14.040
The psychologist Carol Gilligan landed the
hardest blow.

191
00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.300
Kohlberg had built his ladder almost
entirely by studying boys,

192
00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:22.600
and when girls reasoned about moral problems
in the language of care, relationship,

193
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:27.120
and responsibility rather than abstract
rules, the scoring marked them down,

194
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:31.260
as though a morality centred on not
abandoning people were a less grown-up thing

195
00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.120
than a morality centred on principle.

196
00:11:33.896 --> 00:11:35.420
It was not less grown-up.

197
00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:37.780
It was a different dharma's priorities,

198
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.840
mistaken for a lower rung on a universal
ladder.

199
00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:42.620
Even our measuring stick

200
00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.580
for moral maturity had a particular dharma
quietly carved into it.

201
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:52.800
The neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky gives
the screw one more turn.

202
00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.200
The moral convictions we would stake our
lives on,

203
00:11:56.380 --> 00:12:00.860
the ones that feel too plainly, eternally
true to be cultural at all:

204
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:02.960
that slavery is an abomination,

205
00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.100
that the deliberate torture of a child is
unforgivable.

206
00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.960
These feel like bedrock,

207
00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:11.980
like things any decent human in any age
would have felt in their bones.

208
00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:13.540
They are not.

209
00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:15.140
They are recent.

210
00:12:15.883 --> 00:12:19.740
For most of human history most people felt
no such automatic horror,

211
00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.900
and the reason we feel it now, instantly,

212
00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:24.260
in the gut, without having to reason it
out,

213
00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:27.560
is that generations before us did the
hard, contested,

214
00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.540
deliberate moral reasoning and the
dangerous activism

215
00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.920
that slowly retrained the human gut.

216
00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:37.500
Your intuitions, in Sapolsky's phrase, are
"the end products of learning."

217
00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.420
Your guts learned their intuitions.

218
00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.000
The very thing that feels most like
untaught

219
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.800
nature is the most thoroughly taught thing
of all.

220
00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.480
So the first layer of installation is
this:

221
00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.100
more than handing you beliefs to consider,

222
00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:55.180
a dharma tunes the instrument on which you
will feel,

223
00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.400
before you ever reason, what is decent and
what is disgusting,

224
00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.160
and it tunes it so early and so deep

225
00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:04.420
that the tuning feels like the sound of
reality itself.

226
00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.580
We become what we repeatedly do

227
00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:15.440
If morality is mostly the elephant, and
the elephant is trained,

228
00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.140
the obvious next question is: trained how?

229
00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.400
And the answer is the second layer.

230
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.860
Not, in the main, by teaching, but by
drilling.

231
00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.540
The point is as old as Aristotle,

232
00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.100
who saw it clearly more than two thousand
years ago.

233
00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.620
We do not become good by learning the
definition of goodness,

234
00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:38.000
he argued, any more than we become
builders by reading about building.

235
00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.600
We become just by doing just acts,

236
00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:44.540
brave by doing brave acts, temperate by
doing temperate ones.

237
00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.440
Virtue, for Aristotle, is not a belief one
holds in the head but a disposition,

238
00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.860
a settled habit of the whole person,

239
00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.540
grooved into you by repetition until it
runs without effort,

240
00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.380
the way a trained musician plays without
thinking about the notes.

241
00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:06.360
(The famous line often quoted here, "we are
what we repeatedly do; excellence, then,

242
00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:11.640
is not an act but a habit," is in fact a
paraphrase by the historian Will Durant,

243
00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.480
not Aristotle's own words.)

244
00:14:14.953 --> 00:14:18.760
This is why real dharmas are so
astonishingly heavy with practice,

245
00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.640
far heavier than mere belief could ever
require.

246
00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:25.800
The five daily prayers, the fast that
empties the stomach for a month,

247
00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:29.960
the bow, the prostration, the chant
repeated until the words dissolve,

248
00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:33.140
the dietary discipline observed at every
single meal:

249
00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:36.460
the sheer relentless repetition is a
central mechanism.

250
00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.840
Doing the thing ten thousand times grooves
the disposition,

251
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:41.500
and the grooved disposition,

252
00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.740
not the stated belief, is what runs a life
when the mind is elsewhere.

253
00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.380
And ritual does something even bare
repetition cannot.

254
00:14:52.028 --> 00:14:52.980
It bonds.

255
00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.260
More than a century ago,

256
00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.060
the sociologist Émile Durkheim noticed
that

257
00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:01.400
when people move and chant and feel
together in a rite,

258
00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:02.380
something is generated

259
00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:05.349
that he called "collective effervescence,"
a

260
00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:08.540
kind of emotional electricity that fuses a
crowd

261
00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:11.440
into a community and leaves the
participants feeling

262
00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.700
they have touched something larger and
sacred.

263
00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:17.620
That sounds mystical, but it has been
measured in the body,

264
00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.360
in one striking field study,

265
00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:24.080
when anthropologist Dimitris Xygalatas
wired up a Spanish village firewalk:

266
00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:28.020
both the men who walked across the coals,
and the spectators who only watched.

267
00:15:28.658 --> 00:15:31.500
The walkers' hearts raced, as one would
expect.

268
00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:36.840
The startling finding was that the spectators'
hearts raced in synchrony with them,

269
00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:41.320
beat-for-beat, and the synchrony was
tightest between people who loved each other,

270
00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:44.780
so tight that one could read the social
distance between two people

271
00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:46.740
from the similarity of their heartbeats.

272
00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:51.640
The rite was, quite literally, tuning
their bodies to a common rhythm.

273
00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.420
Even the bare form of ritual does work on
the mind.

274
00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.140
Researchers ran a three-month programme of
circle-time games

275
00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:02.140
with two hundred and ten schoolchildren,

276
00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:06.100
in places as different as Slovakia and the
islands of Vanuatu.

277
00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:09.820
The training improved the children's
self-control,

278
00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.780
and through it their ability to delay
gratification;

279
00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.880
and the improvement was larger

280
00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:17.260
when the very same games were framed in
what

281
00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.880
the researchers call a "ritual stance"
rather

282
00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.060
than a practical one.

283
00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:26.220
The stance is nothing more than performing
a precise sequence of actions carefully,

284
00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:30.180
just because that is how it is done, with
no practical purpose at all.

285
00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.280
The bare form did not do the work by
itself,

286
00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:35.840
then; it sharpened what the drilling
achieved:

287
00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:40.100
the discipline of doing it exactly is
itself a training of the will.

288
00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.900
The body, in all of this, leads and the
mind follows.

289
00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:49.500
You do not kneel because you already feel
reverence; very often you kneel first,

290
00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.020
night after night, and the reverence is
what

291
00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.300
grows in the worn groove of the kneeling.

292
00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.300
Not all dharmas drill the same way.

293
00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.320
Anthropologist Harvey Whitehouse has
mapped two very different routes

294
00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:03.680
to the same destination.

295
00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:09.720
One he calls the doctrinal mode: frequent,
calm, low-key repetition,

296
00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:14.000
the daily prayer and the weekly service
and the lesson learned by rote,

297
00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:18.180
which lays the dharma down in ordinary
memory and can hold a vast,

298
00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.040
scattered community in loose agreement for
centuries.

299
00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:26.260
The other he calls the imagistic mode:
rare, shattering ordeal,

300
00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:30.240
the terrifying initiation or the
once-in-a-lifetime rite,

301
00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:33.460
which burns itself into memory as a single
unforgettable event

302
00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:39.020
and welds a small group into something
close to a single self, fused for life.

303
00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.060
The quiet daily groove

304
00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:44.760
and the rare branding ordeal are two
technologies for one task,

305
00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:48.180
installing a disposition so deep it feels
like identity,

306
00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.740
and most dharmas run on some blend of the
two.

307
00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:57.540
If a dharma installs itself through
ritual, repetition, ordeal,

308
00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:02.480
and the disciplined body, what happens to
a dharma that rejects supernatural ritual?

309
00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:06.500
That is the secular project's hardest
practical problem,

310
00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:10.300
and people have begun, self-consciously,
to experiment with it.

311
00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:15.920
There are now godless congregations,
gatherings with names like the Sunday Assembly,

312
00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:19.120
half-jokingly described as Pentecostalism
for atheists.

313
00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:23.000
They borrow the song, the assembly, and
the shared rhythm,

314
00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.280
and drop the doctrine to rebuild the
installation machinery without the gods.

315
00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:31.540
Whether such things can really cultivate
durable dispositions,

316
00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:35.760
or whether the apparatus only bites when
anchored to something held sacred,

317
00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.320
is genuinely unknown.

318
00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.880
All the way into the brain

319
00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:46.500
The installation goes deeper than habit,

320
00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.600
down into the tissue, and here we reach
the third layer,

321
00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:53.360
the one everyone is most excited about,
and the one most oversold.

322
00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.680
The exciting findings are real, though
smaller than they sound.

323
00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.500
When neuroscientists studied Tibetan monks

324
00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:04.760
with tens of thousands of hours of
meditation behind them,

325
00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.060
they recorded patterns of high-frequency,

326
00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:09.620
perfectly synchronised brain activity
"greater

327
00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.360
than any previously reported" in healthy
people.

328
00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:17.660
Hold that result loosely: there were eight
monks, nobody has repeated the study,

329
00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:20.840
and the frequency band it turns on is the
one most easily

330
00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:24.300
contaminated by the electrical activity of
the scalp muscles you use

331
00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:26.120
to frown or clench your jaw.

332
00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:31.140
And the lab work turned up something useful
for thinking about ethics: compassion,

333
00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:35.420
it seems, is not the same thing in the brain
as empathy, and the difference matters.

334
00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:40.480
When the seasoned monk Matthieu Ricard was
asked simply

335
00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:42.220
to feel the suffering of others,

336
00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.500
his brain lit up the ordinary circuitry of
shared pain,

337
00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:46.840
and he found it quickly unbearable,

338
00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:51.480
the way a nurse or a parent can be
hollowed out by absorbing too much hurt.

339
00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.420
But when he switched to trained
compassion,

340
00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.020
a warm, steady wish for others to be well,

341
00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.040
the distress circuitry went quiet

342
00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.220
and the brain's warm reward system came on
instead.

343
00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.080
This is a striking vindication of
something

344
00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:07.780
the contemplative traditions long insisted
on

345
00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:11.700
and that the psychologist Paul Bloom has
argued hard in our own day:

346
00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:17.360
raw empathy, the soaking up of others'
pain, is actually a poor moral guide.

347
00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:21.440
It is exhausting, and it is parochial and
innumerate,

348
00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.740
lavishing itself on the single near
sufferer one can see

349
00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:27.700
while going numb to the distant millions
one cannot.

350
00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:30.860
The dharmic move, to cultivate a calm

351
00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:34.700
and even-handed goodwill rather than to
drown in fellow-feeling,

352
00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.520
is the one the brain rewards.

353
00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:41.140
One might expect that the people most
overwhelmed by

354
00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:44.000
the sight of suffering would be the ones
who help most.

355
00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.520
The opposite is closer to the truth.

356
00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:51.360
Sapolsky points to evidence that people
exposed to another's distress tend

357
00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:54.540
to step in and help when their own heart
rate goes down,

358
00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:58.540
when they steady themselves, rather than
when it spikes in shared panic.

359
00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:03.180
Raw, contagious distress tends to make us
flinch away or freeze,

360
00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:05.200
curled around our own discomfort;

361
00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:09.160
it is the settled, regulated compassion
that frees a person to act.

362
00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:14.420
The traditions that teach us to breathe,
to steady ourselves, and only then to help,

363
00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.820
rather than to dissolve in fellow-feeling,

364
00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:20.780
were being effective, not cold, and the
physiology backs them up.

365
00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:23.820
The popular headline,

366
00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:28.480
that meditation simply "rewires your
brain," outruns the evidence,

367
00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.340
and the real story is more interesting
than the hype.

368
00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:34.420
The most eye-catching claims were about
structure,

369
00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:38.000
that practice visibly thickens the cortex
or grows grey matter.

370
00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:40.440
Early studies suggested as much.

371
00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.420
But when Richard Davidson's own
laboratory,

372
00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.980
one of the founding temples of the whole
field,

373
00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:48.780
ran the rigorous trials that the early
work lacked,

374
00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.940
randomising participants and controlling
properly,

375
00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:54.280
the structural changes did not appear.

376
00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.900
A founding lab failed to replicate the
founding claim,

377
00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:02.140
which is exactly the kind of self-correction
that separates science from sales.

378
00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:03.740
The careful verdict is

379
00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:07.320
that sustained practice reliably changes
how the brain functions,

380
00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.900
especially in attention and stress
regulation,

381
00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.260
while the dramatic claims

382
00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:15.120
that it reshapes the brain's anatomy are,
for now, unproven.

383
00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:19.400
And we should remember that almost any
serious training changes the brain;

384
00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:22.600
the brains of jugglers and taxi drivers
change too.

385
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.020
That the brain adapts

386
00:22:24.020 --> 00:22:27.920
to what we repeatedly do is Aristotle's
point again, not a miracle.

387
00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.080
There is a second silence in this
literature,

388
00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:34.340
and it is the one that matters most for
anyone

389
00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:36.860
who takes up a practice on a book's
recommendation.

390
00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.120
Meditation can hurt people.

391
00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:43.320
When researchers surveyed more than twelve
hundred regular meditators,

392
00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:47.300
a quarter of them reported particularly
unpleasant experiences they attributed

393
00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:48.740
to the practice itself:

394
00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:52.640
anxiety, dread, the floor of the self
giving way,

395
00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.500
dissociation from the world or from their
own bodies.

396
00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.580
Willoughby Britton, who studies these
effects,

397
00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:02.100
argues they are neither rare nor a sign of
doing it wrong;

398
00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:05.820
they are what a powerful technique does to
some people some of the time,

399
00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.560
and the traditions that developed these
methods knew it,

400
00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:12.400
which is why they surrounded them with
teachers and staged them over years.

401
00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:17.020
One review found that of a hundred and
twenty-four published trials of mindfulness,

402
00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:22.240
a hundred and eight reported positive
results, a ratio no honest field produces.

403
00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.600
So the picture is not that meditation is
fraudulent.

404
00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:31.040
It is that a real and useful technology
has been marketed with the risks filed off,

405
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:35.480
and a book arguing that dharmas install
themselves below the level of argument ought

406
00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:38.700
to say plainly that the installation can
go wrong.

407
00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:44.180
A darker finding used to close a circle we
keep drawing,

408
00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:45.760
and I have had to give it up.

409
00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:51.240
The chemistry of human bonding is sold to
us as a "love hormone," oxytocin,

410
00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.700
the warm molecule of trust and connection.

411
00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:58.980
The sophisticated correction, the one I
reached for in earlier drafts of this part,

412
00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:02.140
was that oxytocin is really a parochial
drug:

413
00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:04.380
Carsten De Dreu's experiments appeared

414
00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:08.800
to show it driving what researchers call
"tend-and-defend," the same surge

415
00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:12.860
that deepens devotion to your own people
sharpening suspicion of the stranger.

416
00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:15.940
The fence, written into the bloodstream.

417
00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:18.100
It makes a wonderful sentence.

418
00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:21.860
The evidence, as it stands at the moment,
no longer carries it.

419
00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.320
The first problem is plumbing.

420
00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:29.940
Nearly all of this work squirts oxytocin
up volunteers' noses,

421
00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.940
and the physiologists Gareth Leng and Mike
Ludwig have argued

422
00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:34.560
that almost none of it reaches the brain;

423
00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:38.520
what it reliably does is flood the blood
with far more of the hormone

424
00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:40.660
than a body would ever produce on its own.

425
00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:43.060
The second problem is size.

426
00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:47.320
These are small studies, underpowered in
the statistician's sense,

427
00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:52.480
which is a dependable recipe for results
that shine once and dim on re-examination.

428
00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:56.480
And then there is what De Dreu himself
published in 2024.

429
00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:58.800
Across fifty-one nations,

430
00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:02.320
in Kenyan communities and in a
representative British sample,

431
00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:06.140
people competed harder with members of
their own group than with outsiders,

432
00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.960
whenever resources inside the group felt
scarce.

433
00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:13.840
Biologists studying birds and primates
call this the nasty-neighbour effect.

434
00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:17.400
It is very nearly the opposite of the tidy
story.

435
00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.700
So the fence is real, but the bloodstream
did not put it there.

436
00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:25.800
We met it in the village and again in the
covenant,

437
00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:30.220
and it is built by the ordinary machinery
this part has been describing all along:

438
00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:34.840
habit, belonging, repetition, the slow
grooving of us and them.

439
00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:38.880
That is a less thrilling answer than a
molecule, and a sturdier one.

440
00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.540
Keep the nasty neighbour in mind, though,

441
00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:43.860
because it carries a warning we will need
later.

442
00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:47.580
The fence can run inside a group as
readily as around it.

443
00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:52.000
And because the brain images are so
seductive,

444
00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:56.220
it needs to be said plainly: none of this
makes any dharma true.

445
00:25:56.740 --> 00:26:00.760
A coloured scan showing the neural
signature of compassion no more proves

446
00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:06.240
that compassion is right than a scan of
revulsion proves that a taboo is justified.

447
00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:09.660
The brain can show us that a conviction is
installed,

448
00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:12.580
deeply, physically, beautifully installed.

449
00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.920
It can never show us that the conviction
is correct.

450
00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:19.960
The wiring is a fact about the believer,
not about the world.

451
00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:24.340
Why it feels like truth

452
00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:30.220
The three layers, together, answer the
question we started with.

453
00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.120
Your dharma lives in your moral taste
buds,

454
00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:39.200
in the instantaneous flare of approval or
disgust that arrives long

455
00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.420
before any argument and that your reasoning
mind merely dresses up afterwards.

456
00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.040
It lives in your trained habits,

457
00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:50.160
in dispositions grooved so deep by
repetition that they run on their own,

458
00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:54.700
without consulting you, when you are tired
or frightened or not paying attention.

459
00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:57.740
And it lives in the very tuning of your
brain,

460
00:26:57.740 --> 00:27:00.520
in circuits worn smooth by years of
practice.

461
00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:03.240
One thing is missing from that list:

462
00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:06.860
your conscious, deliberating,
belief-weighing mind,

463
00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:10.080
the part of you that could examine a claim
and find it wanting,

464
00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:12.140
does very little of the installing.

465
00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:16.020
That is not to say it does nothing.

466
00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.000
Reason can choose what to submit itself
to,

467
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.640
can argue an inherited tradition into a
new shape,

468
00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:24.160
and can give or withhold its consent to a
practice

469
00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:26.720
whose work will be done later and lower
down.

470
00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:32.220
The wong sikep of Part 3 did exactly that,
deliberately and out loud,

471
00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:35.080
and the convert and the reformer do it
still.

472
00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:38.900
What reason rarely manages is the
installing itself.

473
00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:43.560
It can walk a person to the training
ground; it cannot do the training.

474
00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.540
That absence, from the deep work, is the
whole secret,

475
00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:51.200
and it is why a dharma feels the way it
does.

476
00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:53.900
It does not feel like a belief one holds

477
00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:56.840
because it was never installed as a belief
in the first place.

478
00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.540
It was installed underneath belief,

479
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:03.100
in the layers that were running long
before the reasoning mind woke up

480
00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:06.180
and that go on running whether the
reasoning mind agrees or not.

481
00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:09.800
Most of what you carry you did not reason
your way into,

482
00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:12.720
which is precisely why it cannot be
reasoned back out,

483
00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:16.380
and why the man at the funeral weeps over
words he is certain are false.

484
00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:21.660
From the inside, a working dharma cannot
feel like an opinion about reality.

485
00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:23.760
It feels like reality,

486
00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:26.100
because it is operating below the level of
the self

487
00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.900
that would be capable of doubting it.

488
00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:32.020
The missing elephant

489
00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:38.140
The argument so far has assumed one thing
without actually saying it:

490
00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.140
that the elephant is there to be trained.

491
00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:44.620
That underneath the rider sits a great
warm animal of instinct,

492
00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.680
the gut that flares at cruelty,

493
00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:50.560
the fear that grips, the empathy that
soaks up another's pain,

494
00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:52.800
waiting to be grooved and tuned.

495
00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:55.620
But in a small fraction of people (how
small

496
00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:57.720
depends on the instrument doing the
counting)

497
00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:00.940
that animal runs faint from very early on.

498
00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:05.620
This is the stretch of the human range we
reach for the word psychopath to name,

499
00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:08.120
and the word is a good deal tidier than
the thing:

500
00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:12.420
it is no formal diagnosis, and the
research treats it as a dimension,

501
00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.440
a direction people lie further or less far
along,

502
00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:17.460
never a separate kind of human being.

503
00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:22.080
What is thin at that end of the range is
not a wicked belief that got installed.

504
00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:26.180
It is the very substrate every dharma
installs into.

505
00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:32.240
When the instrument is thin, the machinery
has almost nothing to work with.

506
00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.980
The moral taste buds barely flare,

507
00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.420
so the flash of care or disgust that is
supposed

508
00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.300
to arrive before reasoning does not
reliably come.

509
00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:44.580
The grooving of habit has far less to
grip, because guilt and fear,

510
00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:47.120
the mortar that sets a conscience, are
running low.

511
00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:50.720
The compassion the monk spent ten thousand
hours warming is,

512
00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.880
in this brain, cool to begin with;

513
00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.260
this is the person on whom the ordinary
apparatus of shame

514
00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.640
and fellow-feeling finds almost nothing to
catch.

515
00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:03.260
The installation engine, so nearly
irresistible in the rest of us,

516
00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:05.620
meets a surface it cannot easily bite.

517
00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.840
The limit case proves the rule from the
far side:

518
00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.660
morality is grooved into an affective
instrument,

519
00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:14.980
and where the instrument is faint, the
grooving barely takes.

520
00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:20.600
The core of that difference is a single
dialled-down setting: fear.

521
00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:23.160
The finding about helping returns here:

522
00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:26.840
the people who run toward the fire are the
ones whose hearts steady;

523
00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:29.060
the useful thing is a fearless calm.

524
00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:33.820
The person whose fear runs low to begin
with has that calm for nothing.

525
00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.820
So the very same low-fear temperament is
the raw

526
00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:39.940
material of the fearless rescuer, the
steady surgeon,

527
00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:42.060
the leader who does not flinch in the
crisis,

528
00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:45.700
and of the cold exploiter who feels
nothing as he uses others.

529
00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:49.300
Which one a life becomes is not fixed by
the temperament;

530
00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:52.620
it is settled by what, if anything, gets
built around it.

531
00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:56.000
Even this is contested, and the caveat
matters:

532
00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:58.240
the fearlessness that does the good may be

533
00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:00.640
the least psychopathic part of the
pattern,

534
00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:03.360
separable from the callousness that does
the harm,

535
00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:06.680
so that "the useful psychopath" may be
less a paradox

536
00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:09.000
than two different things wearing one
name.

537
00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:13.680
Still, the deeper point survives the
caveat.

538
00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:18.980
A dialled-down fear response is, in
itself, neither good nor evil;

539
00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:20.560
it is raw material,

540
00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:24.500
awaiting a dharma (or the absence of one)
to make something of it.

541
00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:28.860
The instrument is as morally blank as the
machinery that would train it.

542
00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:32.460
Which is only a small biological rehearsal
for the larger,

543
00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:34.620
harder thing we have been circling all
along.

544
00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:39.060
The machine has no conscience

545
00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.820
Not one item on the list of what makes a
dharma

546
00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:46.720
powerful has any moral content of its own.

547
00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.980
The grooving of habit will install cruelty
exactly

548
00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:51.840
as faithfully as it installs kindness;

549
00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:56.220
the same patient repetition that shapes a
saint shapes the guard

550
00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:59.220
who has learned to process the condemned
without flinching.

551
00:31:59.660 --> 00:32:01.120
The collective effervescence

552
00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:04.720
that fuses a congregation in love will
fuse a mob in hatred

553
00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:06.700
with the identical electricity.

554
00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:10.560
The belonging that makes us cherish our
own is the same belonging

555
00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:12.780
that teaches us whom to hold at arm's
length.

556
00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:17.600
The installation machinery is, from top to
bottom, morally blank.

557
00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:22.800
It will drive whatever is fed into it down
into the gut, the muscle,

558
00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.120
and the brain with the same quiet
efficiency,

559
00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:28.680
and label the result, in every case,
"obvious truth."

560
00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:34.520
And because it works below awareness, you
cannot feel, from the inside,

561
00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:38.660
the difference between a dharma that is
guiding you and one that has captured you.

562
00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:41.120
Both feel exactly the same.

563
00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:44.300
Both feel like simply seeing the world as
it is.

564
00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:49.380
The depth that lets a great dharma make a
person good is the identical depth

565
00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.880
that lets a corrupt one make a person
monstrous

566
00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:55.100
while leaving them serenely certain of
their own decency.

567
00:32:56.810 --> 00:33:01.740
We have spent four parts, in admiration,
watching how dharmas hold us together,

568
00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.400
and this one watching how they get inside
us.

569
00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:08.880
If this machinery runs beneath reason,
installs whatever it is given,

570
00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:11.720
and feels like truth no matter what it has
installed,

571
00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:16.560
then the most pressing question is, not
how a dharma works, but how it goes wrong.

